---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = 'Turf — Contact';
const description = 'Talk to the Turf team — design-partner inquiries, commercial licensing, and security reports.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="subhero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Contact</p>
      <h1>Talk to us.</h1>
      <p class="lede">We’re a small team building Turf in the open. The fastest way to reach us is
        email — we read every message.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>General</h2>
      <p>Questions, feedback, or just want to compare notes on governing infrastructure AI?
        Write to <a href="mailto:contact@turf.build">contact@turf.build</a>.</p>

      <h2>Design partners</h2>
      <p>We’re looking for teams working on layered infrastructure, policy gates, and AI under real
        governance constraints. Design partners get white-glove onboarding, direct roadmap influence,
        and discounted commercial terms while we build together.
        <a href="mailto:contact@turf.build?subject=Design%20partner">Start the conversation →</a></p>

      <h2>Commercial licensing</h2>
      <p>Planning to run Turf in production? Commercial licensing is coming — tell us about your use
        case at <a href="mailto:contact@turf.build?subject=Commercial">contact@turf.build</a>.</p>

      <h2>Security</h2>
      <p>Found a vulnerability? Please report it privately to
        <a href="mailto:security@turf.build">security@turf.build</a> rather than opening a public issue.</p>

      <h2>Stay in the loop</h2>
      <p>Release notes, new capabilities, and design-partner openings — subscribe from the footer of
        any page, or follow <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/turfbuild/">Turf on LinkedIn</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt" id="about">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> About us</p>
      <h2>Meet the founders.</h2>
      <p class="lede">We’re Eron and Tonya Wright, co-founders of Turf LLC, a family-run business
        operating out of Depoe Bay, Oregon, USA. We started Turf because we think the IaC community is
        ready for a new Terraform®-class tool, one with semantic reasoning and built for integrators.</p>

      <div class="founders">
        <div class="founder">
          <img class="headshot" src="/eron-wright.jpg" alt="Eron Wright, co-founder of Turf"
            width="150" height="188" loading="lazy" />
          <div class="prose">
            <h3>Eron Wright — Co-founder</h3>
            <p>Eron has spent his career at the foundations of cloud infrastructure. As a former
              principal engineer at <a href="https://www.pulumi.com/">Pulumi</a>, he was a developer and
              maintainer of the Pulumi Kubernetes Operator, Pulumi providers, and the Pulumi Go Provider
              SDK.</p>
            <p>Before Pulumi, he built SaaS data-streaming platforms based on Kubernetes and Apache
              Flink, and earlier still he helped shape Microsoft’s first Infrastructure-as-a-Service
              offering as part of the core Azure team. Across all of it, one thread holds: a focus on
              making powerful infrastructure safer and easier to operate.</p>
            <p>At Turf, Eron leads the engineering team — turning years of hands-on IaC experience into a
              reconciliation engine that reasons about your infrastructure instead of blindly grinding
              through a dependency graph.</p>
            <p><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/eronwright/">Connect with Eron on LinkedIn →</a></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="founder">
          <img class="headshot" src="/tonya-wright.jpg" alt="Tonya Wright, co-founder of Turf"
            width="150" height="188" loading="lazy" />
          <div class="prose">
            <h3>Tonya Wright — Co-founder</h3>
            <p>Tonya is the founder and photographer behind Nest &amp; Love Photography, a business
              she’s run on the Oregon Coast for nearly a decade. A commercial and lifestyle photographer
              known for moody, cinematic imagery — blending photojournalism with a nostalgic,
              film-inspired aesthetic — she has documented more than 200 weddings and 300 couples’
              sessions, and created brand work for dozens of small businesses. Her photography has been
              published in multiple magazines, including two covers.</p>
            <p>Running a client-driven business for that long takes exactly the instincts a young company
              needs: earning trust, understanding what people truly want, and delivering it on time and
              with care. That customer obsession is Tonya’s north star, and it’s the standard she holds
              Turf to. Where Eron thinks about what Turf <em>can</em> do, Tonya keeps us honest about
              what our customers actually <em>need</em>.</p>
            <p>She also brings a deep commitment to community. Through Nest &amp; Love she has donated
              thousands of dollars, hundreds of hours, and more than 90 photo sessions to nonprofits,
              schools, and neighbors who deserved to be seen — supporting causes from ocean conservation
              with the Marine Mammal Institute to the West Columbia Gorge Humane Society and Habitat for
              Humanity. That same generosity of spirit is one we want at the heart of how Turf treats its
              customers and its community.</p>
            <p>Tonya handles daily operations at Turf.</p>
            <p><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/tonya-wright-932414159/">Connect with Tonya on LinkedIn →</a></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose why">
        <h2>Why we built Turf</h2>
        <p>Every day, we trust infrastructure tools to make changes to our most important environments
          and resources. The risk of disruption and data loss is frankly understated, and increases as
          these tools are hidden below layers of automation, simply because visibility decreases.</p>
        <p>We believe reasoning LLMs, used safely and deliberately, bring much-needed supervision to
          infrastructure ops. We want to “shift-right” the semantic knowledge about your resources and
          policies towards the point where infrastructure is actually touched. Meanwhile, users want to
          “shift-left” their infrastructure configurations using Terraform modules and HCL. LLMs are
          increasingly responsible for writing those configurations — how about implementing them?</p>
        <p>At the same time, users and organizations don’t want click-ops at AI speed. Turf drives
          infrastructure change using Terraform plans that are subject to human approval, policy checks,
          and more. The LLM serves as the executor of the plan, following a strict TODO list and
          observing the effects.</p>
        <p>We’re thrilled to bring this modern approach to the IaC community — and we’d love for you to
          be part of it. Have a question, or just want to talk shop?
          <a href="mailto:contact@turf.build">Get in touch</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .founders { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: clamp(28px, 4vw, 44px); margin-top: 30px; }
  .founder { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 150px 1fr; gap: clamp(20px, 3vw, 36px); align-items: start; }
  .founder .prose { max-width: 62ch; }
  .founder .prose h3 { margin-top: 0; }
  .headshot {
    width: 150px; aspect-ratio: 4 / 5; object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: 16px; border: 1px solid var(--hair);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow); background: var(--panel-2);
  }
  .why { max-width: 70ch; margin-top: clamp(30px, 4vw, 48px); }
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .founder { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .headshot { width: 128px; }
  }
</style>
